  "worked_solution": "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{6x+10}=x+3,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+10} & =x+3 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6x+10})}^{2} & =(x+3)^{2} \\\\ 6x+10 & ={x}^{2}+6x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(-1)+10} & \\overset{?}{=}-1+3 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=-1$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(1)+10} & \\overset{?}{=}1+3 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=1$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{6x+10}=x+3$  is $x=\\pm 1.$",
